"# Chapter 10: The Roster & Modding Voices

You've built instruments from scratch—oscillator, harmonics, filter, envelope, body, noise—and you know what each knob does. But you don't want to wire every flute from first principles every time you need a flute. You want a *roster*: a collection of voices you can call by name, play a phrase through, and modify when the stock version isn't quite right.

This chapter is the tour: what instruments we have, how they're grouped into families, and how to *mod* a voice—make small, surgical changes without rewriting the whole definition. We'll play one phrase through five instruments, then take one instrument and mod it three ways. By the end, you'll know how to call up any voice, swap it for another, and tweak it when you need a brighter pluck or a softer body.

---

## The Roster

An instrument is a *profile*: a bundle of settings that together define a voice. Conceptually, `define-instrument` names a bundle and `part` plays notes through it.

> **A note on this chapter.** Base Motoi's audio surface plays a `tone`, a `chord`, and a `note`. It does NOT expose the roster/modding verbs `define-instrument`, `voice`, `part`, `mod-instrument`, `wave`, `noise`, `filter`, `adsr`, `partials`, or `add-formant`. So every instrument-profile and mod example below is illustrative *pseudocode* shown in `text` fences — it describes how a voice bundle would be assembled and modded. The runnable anchor is the raw `tone` and the craft/movement `note` reaches, which use only real verbs.

Here's a simple tone played through a sine wave—no instrument, just the raw oscillator:

```scheme
(tone 440 0.5)
```

The same note played through a *named voice* would look like the pseudocode below — the voice wraps the oscillator, the part wraps the notes:

```text
(define-instrument 'plain-sine
  (wave 'sine))

(part (voice 'plain-sine)
  '((440 0.5)))
```

The voice wraps the oscillator. The part wraps the notes. This separation—voice from phrase—lets you swap one for the other without rewriting both.

---

## Families

Instruments cluster into families by how they make sound:

- **Plucked**: energy at the start, then decay (guitar, harp, harpsichord, kalimba)
- **Bowed**: sustained energy, slow attack (violin, cello, saw-synth pad)
- **Blown**: breath noise + resonance (flute, clarinet, brass, pan pipes)
- **Struck**: percussive, often inharmonic (piano, marimba, bells, glockenspiel)
- **Electronic**: pure waveforms, no acoustic model (sine pad, square lead, sawtooth bass)

Let's hear a plucked voice:

```text
(define-instrument 'guitar-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2000 :q 1.0))

(part (voice 'guitar-pluck)
  '((330 0.6)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

And a bowed voice:

```text
(define-instrument 'cello-bow
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.7 0.5 0.3 0.2))
  (adsr :a 0.2 :d 0.1 :s 0.7 :r 0.3)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1200 :q 2.0))

(part (voice 'cello-bow)
  '((220 1.0)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The difference is in the envelope: the pluck has zero sustain (it dies after the decay), the bow has high sustain (it holds as long as you hold the note).

---

## One Phrase, Five Instruments

Here's a short melodic phrase—four notes, no rhythm complexity:

```text
(define melody
  '((440 0.3) (494 0.3) (523 0.3) (587 0.4)))

(part (wave 'sine) melody)
```

Now we'll play it through five different voices. Each voice is a different instrument family.

### 1. Sine Pad (Electronic)

```text
(define-instrument 'sine-pad
  (wave 'sine)
  (adsr :a 0.1 :d 0.0 :s 1.0 :r 0.2))

(part (voice 'sine-pad)
  '((440 0.3) (494 0.3) (523 0.3) (587 0.4)))
```

*[needs: adsr]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope -->

### 2. Plucked Harp

The harp has a bright attack that dies away — no sustain, no hold. Triangle wave with a few upper partials gives the string a bit of bite.

```text
(define-instrument 'harp-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.6 0.3 0.15))
  (adsr :a 0.005 :d 0.4 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 3000 :q 0.7))

(part (voice 'harp-pluck)
  '((440 0.3) (494 0.3) (523 0.3) (587 0.4)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The tight `:d 0.4 :s 0.0 :r 0.0` envelope is what makes it a *pluck* and not a *pad* — a bowed string would hold the sustain instead.

### 3. Blown Flute

Now switch to a flute — the difference is *breath*. A little white noise mixed in gives the airy, wooden character, and a bandpass filter shapes the mouthpiece tone.

```text
(define-instrument 'wooden-flute
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.4 0.2))
  (noise 'white :level 0.05)
  (adsr :a 0.08 :d 0.05 :s 0.8 :r 0.15)
  (filter 'bandpass :cutoff 1800 :q 3.0))

(part (voice 'wooden-flute)
  '((440 0.3) (494 0.3) (523 0.3) (587 0.4)))
```

*[needs: noise, adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: noise generator, adsr envelope, filter verb -->

That `noise 'white :level 0.05` is the whole difference between an electronic sine and a wooden flute — the breath sound sells it.

### 4. Struck Marimba

The marimba is *struck* — almost no attack time, quick decay, and formants that shape it into a wooden bar rather than a bell.

```text
(define-instrument 'marimba-mallet
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25 0.12))
  (adsr :a 0.003 :d 0.15 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (add-formant 400 '(800 1200)))

(part (voice 'marimba-mallet)
  '((440 0.3) (494 0.3) (523 0.3) (587 0.4)))
```

*[needs: adsr, add-formant]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, formant bank -->

The `add-formant 400 '(800 1200)` gives the marimba its hollow wooden resonance — remove those formants and it turns into a plain thump.

### 5. Bowed Violin

```text
(define-instrument 'violin-bow
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.3))
  (noise 'white :level 0.02)
  (adsr :a 0.15 :d 0.1 :s 0.75 :r 0.25)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2500 :q 1.5))

(part (voice 'violin-bow)
  '((440 0.3) (494 0.3) (523 0.3) (587 0.4)))
```

*[needs: noise, adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: noise generator, adsr envelope, filter verb -->

Same notes, five timbres. The melody doesn't change; the *voice* does.

---

## Modding a Voice

You have a voice, but you want it *brighter*, or *softer*, or with a *longer decay*. You could copy the whole `define-instrument` block and edit one line—but that duplicates everything. Instead, you *mod* the voice: start with the base, apply a delta.

`mod-instrument` takes a base voice and a set of overrides:

```text
(mod-instrument 'base-voice
  :key 'new-value
  :another-key 'new-value)
```

Only the specified keys change. Everything else inherits from the base.

---

### Base Voice: A Simple Pluck

```text
(define-instrument 'basic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.25 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1800 :q 1.0))

(part (voice 'basic-pluck)
  '((330 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

### Mod 1: Brighter (Higher Cutoff)

```text
(define-instrument 'basic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.25 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1800 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'bright-pluck
  (mod-instrument 'basic-pluck
    :cutoff 3500))

(part (voice 'bright-pluck)
  '((330 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

Only the cutoff changed. The envelope, partials, and waveform stay the same.

### Mod 2: Longer Decay

```text
(define-instrument 'basic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.25 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1800 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'long-pluck
  (mod-instrument 'basic-pluck
    :d 0.6))

(part (voice 'long-pluck)
  '((330 0.8)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

The decay stretches from 0.25 to 0.6 seconds. Everything else inherits.

### Mod 3: Softer Body (Lower Resonance)

```text
(define-instrument 'basic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.25 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1800 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'soft-pluck
  (mod-instrument 'basic-pluck
    :q 0.4))

(part (voice 'soft-pluck)
  '((330 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

The Q (resonance) drops from 1.0 to 0.4, making the filter less peaky—softer, rounder.

---

## The Roster in Action

Let's define a small roster of voices across families, then play a phrase through each.

```text
(define-instrument 'bell
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.67 0.5 0.4))
  (adsr :a 0.002 :d 0.8 :s 0.0 :r 0.0))

(define-instrument 'organ
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.33 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.05 :d 0.0 :s 1.0 :r 0.1))

(define-instrument 'bass
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.7 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.1 :s 0.3 :r 0.1)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 400 :q 2.0))

(part (voice 'bell)
  '((523 0.4) (587 0.4) (659 0.6)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The bell rings a bright high phrase — quick attack, long decay, no sustain. Its inharmonic-leaning partials give it that clear crystal shimmer.

```text
(define-instrument 'organ
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.33 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.05 :d 0.0 :s 1.0 :r 0.1))

(part (voice 'organ)
  '((523 0.4) (587 0.4) (659 0.6)))
```

*[needs: adsr]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope -->

The organ holds each note flat — full sustain, zero decay — because pipes keep sounding as long as air keeps flowing. That's the shape of a sustained voice.

```text
(define-instrument 'bass
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.7 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.1 :s 0.3 :r 0.1)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 400 :q 2.0))

(part (voice 'bass)
  '((131 0.8) (147 0.8)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The bass sits low with a sawtooth wave and a tight lowpass cutoff at 400 Hz — that filter is what makes it *bass* instead of a buzzy synth lead.

Three voices, three timbres. Same roster, different context.

---

## Roster: Plucked Family

Let's define several plucked instruments and hear the differences.

### Guitar

The acoustic guitar has the pluck's short bright attack, but with formants that mimic the wooden body's resonance. Those formants at 220/400/900 Hz are the body cavity coloring the tone.

```text
(define-instrument 'acoustic-guitar
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.6 0.4 0.25 0.15))
  (adsr :a 0.008 :d 0.35 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2200 :q 1.2)
  (add-formant 220 '(400 900)))

(part (voice 'acoustic-guitar)
  '((330 0.5) (370 0.5) (392 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, add-formant]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, formant bank -->

The formant bank is what gives the guitar its "wooden box" character. Strip those formants and it turns back into a plain triangle-wave pluck.

### Harp

A concert harp has a longer decay than a guitar — the strings ring for half a second or more — and a higher, gentler filter cutoff for that airy shimmer.

```text
(define-instrument 'concert-harp
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.3 0.2 0.1))
  (adsr :a 0.003 :d 0.5 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 3500 :q 0.6))

(part (voice 'concert-harp)
  '((523 0.4) (587 0.4) (659 0.4) (698 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The 0.5-second decay is the harp signature — longer than a guitar, shorter than a bell — and the low-Q lowpass keeps it smooth instead of resonant.

### Harpsichord

The harpsichord plucks metal strings with a quill — bright, snappy, no sustain. A sawtooth wave with strong upper partials and a highpass filter thins out the low end to match.

```text
(define-instrument 'harpsichord
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4))
  (adsr :a 0.002 :d 0.15 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'highpass :cutoff 800 :q 0.5))

(part (voice 'harpsichord)
  '((294 0.25) (330 0.25) (370 0.25) (392 0.3)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The highpass at 800 Hz cutting out the low frequencies is the classic Baroque brightness — that's why a harpsichord sits above a bass line instead of muddling it.

### Kalimba

```text
(define-instrument 'kalimba
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.4 0.15))
  (adsr :a 0.005 :d 0.6 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (add-formant 440 '(1200 2400)))

(part (voice 'kalimba)
  '((523 0.35) (659 0.35) (784 0.35) (880 0.4)))
```

*[needs: adsr, add-formant]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, formant bank -->

Each plucked voice has zero sustain (`:s 0.0`)—the note dies after the decay. But decay time, filter cutoff, and harmonic content vary, giving each its character.

---

## Roster: Bowed Family

Bowed instruments sustain. The envelope has a high sustain level and a slow attack.

### Violin

A bowed violin has a slower attack than a plucked string — the bow takes about a tenth of a second to bite. Sawtooth wave with rich harmonics and a whisper of bow-noise sells the friction.

```text
(define-instrument 'solo-violin
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.9 0.7 0.5 0.4 0.3))
  (noise 'white :level 0.03)
  (adsr :a 0.12 :d 0.08 :s 0.8 :r 0.2)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2800 :q 1.8))

(part (voice 'solo-violin)
  '((440 0.8) (494 0.8) (523 0.9)))
```

*[needs: noise, adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: noise generator, adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The high sustain (`:s 0.8`) is what makes it *bowed*: the note holds for as long as the bow moves, unlike a pluck that dies immediately.

### Cello

The cello is the violin's deeper cousin — even slower attack, warmer filter, lower notes. Same sawtooth-plus-lowpass recipe, just tuned an octave down.

```text
(define-instrument 'solo-cello
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.3))
  (adsr :a 0.18 :d 0.1 :s 0.75 :r 0.3)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1500 :q 2.2))

(part (voice 'solo-cello)
  '((220 1.0) (247 1.0) (262 1.2)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

The lower cutoff (1500 vs. the violin's 2800) is what makes the cello sound *warm* rather than *bright* — the same instrument family, shifted darker.

### Synth Pad

```text
(define-instrument 'warm-pad
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.33 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.3 :d 0.2 :s 0.9 :r 0.5)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1200 :q 0.8))

(part (voice 'warm-pad)
  '((330 1.5) (392 1.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

Pads have very slow attacks (0.3s) and long releases—they swell and fade.

---

## Roster: Blown Family

Blown instruments mix tone with breath noise. The filter is often a bandpass, and the envelope has a moderate attack.

### Wooden Flute

```text
(define-instrument 'wood-flute
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.3 0.15))
  (noise 'white :level 0.06)
  (adsr :a 0.09 :d 0.05 :s 0.75 :r 0.12)
  (filter 'bandpass :cutoff 1600 :q 4.0))

(part (voice 'wood-flute)
  '((523 0.6) (587 0.6) (659 0.7)))
```

*[needs: noise, adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: noise generator, adsr envelope, filter verb -->

### Clarinet

```text
(define-instrument 'clarinet
  (wave 'square)
  (partials '(1.0 0.0 0.3 0.0 0.15))
  (noise 'white :level 0.04)
  (adsr :a 0.07 :d 0.05 :s 0.8 :r 0.15)
  (filter 'bandpass :cutoff 1200 :q 5.0))

(part (voice 'clarinet)
  '((392 0.7) (440 0.7) (494 0.8)))
```

*[needs: noise, adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: noise generator, adsr envelope, filter verb -->

Clarinets emphasize odd harmonics (square wave base), with a narrow bandpass.

### Pan Pipes

```text
(define-instrument 'pan-pipes
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.2))
  (noise 'white :level 0.08)
  (adsr :a 0.05 :d 0.03 :s 0.7 :r 0.1)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2000 :q 1.5))

(part (voice 'pan-pipes)
  '((659 0.5) (698 0.5) (784 0.6)))
```

*[needs: noise, adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: noise generator, adsr envelope, filter verb -->

More breath noise, fewer harmonics—airier.

---

## Roster: Struck Family

Struck instruments have very short attacks and often include inharmonic partials (bells, glockenspiel) or rich, decaying spectra (piano, marimba).

### Piano

```text
(define-instrument 'upright-piano
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.6 0.4 0.25 0.15 0.1))
  (adsr :a 0.003 :d 0.4 :s 0.2 :r 0.3)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 3000 :q 0.9))

(part (voice 'upright-piano)
  '((262 0.5) (330 0.5) (392 0.6)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

### Marimba

The marimba's wooden bars ring briefly then die. A sine wave with modest partials, an almost-instant attack, and formants at 440/800/1600 Hz for the wooden body cavity.

```text
(define-instrument 'wood-marimba
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.4 0.2 0.1))
  (adsr :a 0.002 :d 0.2 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (add-formant 440 '(800 1600)))

(part (voice 'wood-marimba)
  '((330 0.4) (392 0.4) (440 0.4) (494 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, add-formant]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, formant bank -->

Those wooden-body formants are what separate a marimba from a glockenspiel — same struck envelope, but the resonant cavity gives it warmth instead of shine.

### Glockenspiel

```text
(define-instrument 'glockenspiel
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.8 0.5 0.3))
  (adsr :a 0.001 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'highpass :cutoff 1500 :q 0.7))

(part (voice 'glockenspiel)
  '((1047 0.3) (1175 0.3) (1319 0.3) (1397 0.4)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

Glockenspiels are bright (high cutoff, highpass filter) and ring briefly.

### Tubular Bell

```text
(define-instrument 'tubular-bell
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.56 0.92 0.51 0.67))
  (adsr :a 0.005 :d 1.5 :s 0.0 :r 0.0))

(part (voice 'tubular-bell)
  '((523 1.8)))
```

*[needs: adsr]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope -->

Inharmonic partials (the ratios aren't integer multiples) give bells their distinctive clang.

---

## Roster: Electronic Family

Pure synthesis—no attempt to model an acoustic instrument.

### Square Lead

```text
(define-instrument 'square-lead
  (wave 'square)
  (partials '(1.0 0.0 0.33 0.0 0.2))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.1 :s 0.5 :r 0.1)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1800 :q 1.5))

(part (voice 'square-lead)
  '((440 0.4) (494 0.4) (523 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

### Sawtooth Bass

```text
(define-instrument 'saw-bass
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.8 0.6 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.008 :d 0.08 :s 0.4 :r 0.08)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 350 :q 3.0))

(part (voice 'saw-bass)
  '((110 0.6) (123 0.6) (131 0.7)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

High Q (3.0) makes the filter resonant—classic acid bass sound.

### Sine Lead

```text
(define-instrument 'sine-lead
  (wave 'sine)
  (adsr :a 0.02 :d 0.05 :s 0.6 :r 0.15))

(part (voice 'sine-lead)
  '((659 0.4) (698 0.4) (784 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope -->

Pure sine, no harmonics—clean, minimal.

---

## Modding in Practice: One Voice, Many Flavors

Let's take a single base voice and mod it several ways to show the range.

### Base: Generic Pluck

```text
(define-instrument 'generic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2000 :q 1.0))

(part (voice 'generic-pluck)
  '((440 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

### Mod: Dark (Low Cutoff)

To darken a pluck we drop the filter cutoff — same voice, but with the highs rolled off. The base is redeclared here just so the mod has something to attach to.

```text
(define-instrument 'generic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2000 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'dark-pluck
  (mod-instrument 'generic-pluck
    :cutoff 800))

(part (voice 'dark-pluck)
  '((440 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

Cutoff dropped from 2000 down to 800 — that's it. The envelope, waveform, and partials all inherit unchanged; the mod is a one-line delta.

### Mod: Metallic (High Q)

A high-Q filter creates a sharp resonant peak — the classic "metallic ring" sound. Same base voice, only the Q is bumped up.

```text
(define-instrument 'generic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2000 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'metal-pluck
  (mod-instrument 'generic-pluck
    :q 4.0))

(part (voice 'metal-pluck)
  '((440 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

Q jumped from 1.0 to 4.0 — one knob for the whole metallic character. The filter now peaks sharply at cutoff instead of rolling smoothly.

### Mod: Soft (Fewer Harmonics, Low Q)

To soften a voice we can adjust two knobs at once: strip out upper partials so it sounds duller, and drop Q so the filter smooths rather than peaks.

```text
(define-instrument 'generic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2000 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'soft-pluck
  (mod-instrument 'generic-pluck
    :partials '(1.0 0.3)
    :q 0.5))

(part (voice 'soft-pluck)
  '((440 0.5)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

Two overrides, two effects — fewer harmonics thin the tone; lower Q rounds off the filter. Mods can bundle several changes into one delta.

### Mod: Short (Fast Decay)

Shortening a pluck is a one-knob move: drop the decay time. The `:d 0.08` mod means the note lasts about a tenth of a second before it dies.

```text
(define-instrument 'generic-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2000 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'short-pluck
  (mod-instrument 'generic-pluck
    :d 0.08))

(part (voice 'short-pluck)
  '((440 0.3)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

Decay went from 0.3 to 0.08 seconds — the whole pluck now flashes and vanishes. Same voice, but *staccato* instead of *sostenuto*.

Five flavors from one base. Each mod changes one or two parameters; everything else inherits.

---

## Swapping Voices Mid-Phrase

You can play different sections of a phrase through different voices by splitting the phrase into parts.

```text
(define-instrument 'harp-pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.6 0.3 0.15))
  (adsr :a 0.005 :d 0.4 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 3000 :q 0.7))

(define-instrument 'cello-bow
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.7 0.5 0.3 0.2))
  (adsr :a 0.2 :d 0.1 :s 0.7 :r 0.3)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1200 :q 2.0))

(part (voice 'harp-pluck)
  '((440 0.4) (494 0.4)))

(part (voice 'cello-bow)
  '((523 0.8) (587 0.8)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

First two notes are harp, next two are cello. Same melody, different timbres mid-stream.

---

## Building a Mod Chain

You can mod a mod—apply deltas on top of deltas.

```text
(define-instrument 'base-saw
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.7 0.5))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.2 :s 0.3 :r 0.1)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1500 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'bright-saw
  (mod-instrument 'base-saw
    :cutoff 2500))

(define-instrument 'bright-long-saw
  (mod-instrument 'bright-saw
    :d 0.5
    :r 0.3))

(part (voice 'bright-long-saw)
  '((220 0.8)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter, mod-instrument]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb, mod-instrument macro -->

`bright-saw` inherits from `base-saw` and raises the cutoff. `bright-long-saw` inherits from `bright-saw` and lengthens the decay and release. Each mod builds on the previous.

---

## Family Tour: A Progression Through Families

Let's play a simple chord progression, changing the voice family on each chord.

```text
(define-instrument 'pluck
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2200 :q 1.0))

(define-instrument 'pad
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.33))
  (adsr :a 0.2 :d 0.0 :s 1.0 :r 0.4))

(define-instrument 'bell
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.6 0.5 0.3))
  (adsr :a 0.003 :d 0.7 :s 0.0 :r 0.0))

(part (voice 'pluck)
  '((330 0.5) (392 0.5) (440 0.5)))

(part (voice 'pad)
  '((349 1.2) (415 1.2)))

(part (voice 'bell)
  '((370 0.6) (440 0.6) (523 0.7)))
```

*[needs: adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope, filter verb -->

Three families, three timbres, one progression.

---

## Layering Voices

You can play the *same* notes through *multiple* voices at once—layering.

```text
(define-instrument 'soft-pad
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.3))
  (adsr :a 0.3 :d 0.0 :s 1.0 :r 0.5))

(define-instrument 'bell-layer
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.5 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.005 :d 0.6 :s 0.0 :r 0.0))

(define melody '((440 1.0) (494 1.0) (523 1.2)))

(part (voice 'soft-pad) melody)
(part (voice 'bell-layer) melody)
```

*[needs: adsr]*  
<!-- BUILD: adsr envelope -->

Both voices play the same notes. The pad sustains, the bell rings and decays. The combination creates a richer texture.

---

## Roster Snapshot: Quick Reference

Here's a compact roster you might keep on hand—one voice per family, ready to call.

```text
(define-instrument 'guitar
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.6 0.4 0.25))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.3 :s 0.0 :r 0.0)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2200 :q 1.2))

(define-instrument 'violin
  (wave 'sawtooth)
  (partials '(1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4))
  (adsr :a 0.15 :d 0.1 :s 0.75 :r 0.25)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 2500 :q 1.5))

(define-instrument 'flute
  (wave 'sine)
  (partials '(1.0 0.3))
  (noise 'white :level 0.05)
  (adsr :a 0.08 :d 0.05 :s 0.8 :r 0.15)
  (filter 'bandpass :cutoff 1800 :q 3.0))

(define-instrument 'piano
  (wave 'triangle)
  (partials '(1.0 0.6 0.4 0.25 0.15))
  (adsr :a 0.003 :d 0.4 :s 0.2 :r 0.3)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 3000 :q 0.9))

(define-instrument 'synth-lead
  (wave 'square)
  (partials '(1.0 0.0 0.33 0.0 0.2))
  (adsr :a 0.01 :d 0.1 :s 0.5 :r 0.1)
  (filter 'lowpass :cutoff 1800 :q 1.5))
```

*[needs: noise, adsr, filter]*  
<!-- BUILD: noise generator, adsr envelope, filter verb -->

